.claude
lawha-data

# The filled-in copy of lawha.env.example. It holds LAWHA_MASTER_PASSWORD —
# a password that signs in as any account — and LAWHA_ADMIN_PASSWORD, so it is
# equivalent to the database itself. The example file stays committed; this one
# never does.
lawha.env

# ...and the timestamped copies a hand-edit leaves behind. The rule above is an
# exact filename, so `lawha.env.bak-20260814-074937` was NOT covered by it and
# was committable — holding the same master password as the original. The `/.env`
# rule below already learned this lesson and grew a `/.env.bak-*` sibling; this
# one had not, and the gap only showed up when a backup was actually taken.
lawha.env.bak-*
lawha.env.*.bak
lawha.env.save

# Compose's own interpolation file, which holds this machine's addresses —
# LAWHA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN and optionally LAWHA_DATA_DIR. It has to be a separate
# file from lawha.env: compose interpolates `${...}` from the shell and from
# ./.env ONLY, never from an `env_file:`, which reaches the container and
# nothing else. Machine-specific, so it is not committed.
#
# Note this is /.env at the root only — .env.development and .env.production
# are Vite's, are shared rather than local, and stay committed.
/.env

# Timestamped copies of the above, which `run.sh` and hand-edits leave behind.
# `/.env` matches that exact name and nothing else, so a backup of it was
# committable — and the next one to be made might hold a filled-in
# NGROK_AUTHTOKEN rather than two dead variables.
/.env.bak-*

# Playwright visual-regression suite (roadmap #25).
# Baseline screenshots under e2e/**/*-snapshots/ are NOT ignored — they are
# the committed baseline. Only run artifacts and the shared-account session
# are.
/test-results
/playwright-report
/playwright/.cache
/blob-report
/e2e/.auth

# Local code-intelligence indexes. Machine-specific, rebuilt on demand, and
# written by whichever tool the session happens to use — so all of them are
# listed rather than the current favourite. `.infigraph/` alone is 9.5MB of
# binary index and its MCP server rewrites it in the background, which is how
# it turned up staged during an unrelated commit.
graphify-out/
.infigraph/
.serena/

# Self-signed dev certificates for LAN testing (machine-specific).
certs/

# Subagent-driven development scratch: ledgers, briefs, review packages.
.superpowers/

# One deployment's own facts: its public URL, its administrator's username, which
# of its protections are switched off. This is the material that used to sit in
# `instruction.md` §13 — a TRACKED file — where publishing the repository would
# have handed a reader a live endpoint and told them rate limiting was off.
#
# The pattern is `*.local.md`, not one filename, so the next person who writes
# their deployment down gets the same protection without having to know this
# comment exists.
*.local.md

# The live database and its write-ahead log, wherever they are put. `lawha-data`
# above only covers the default location; LAWHA_DB_PATH can point anywhere, and a
# stray copy taken while debugging lands wherever the shell happened to be.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Backup artefacts, and the key that opens them. `*.db` above stops at the
# plaintext database and covers none of what the encryption procedure produces —
# while `docs/configuration.md` says `age-keygen -o lawha-backup.key` and
# `restore … --identity ./lawha-backup.key`, both repo-relative, so following
# that procedure from this directory drops an age PRIVATE key into the working
# tree with nothing to stop `git add -A` taking it.
#
# The archive is the worse half. LAWHA_BACKUP_CONFIG mirrors `lawha.env` and
# `certs/` into it (ADR 0020), so one `.age` or `.tar` holds every board, the
# master password and the CA private key at once — and the docs say plainly that
# losing the age key loses every backup, which makes it the single most valuable
# file this repository can be standing next to.
*.age
*.key
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
key-material/

# The local issue/spec drafting area `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` describes.
# It does not exist yet, and it is ignored BEFORE it does: the workflow that
# creates it writes half-formed tickets and internal notes, and the first one
# would otherwise have arrived as a tracked file in a public repository.
.scratch/

# The working brief and the process material behind it. Kept on disk, not
# published: `CLAUDE.md` is the contributor/agent brief, `docs/superpowers/`
# holds the design specs and implementation plans features were built from, and
# `docs/agents/` the conventions an agent follows here. Useful to whoever works
# in this checkout; noise to somebody who just wants to run Lawha.
#
# What a reader needs instead is already published: the 25 invariants live in
# `docs/lawha-roadmap.md` §2 and every decision has an ADR in `docs/adr/`.
CLAUDE.md
# `AGENTS.md` is byte-identical to CLAUDE.md — `gitnexus analyze` writes both
# from one template. Ignoring only the first would publish the second by
# accident, which is the opposite of what the paragraph above decided.
AGENTS.md
# `SKILL.md` is the same class of artefact: which skill to reach for in this
# checkout. Useful here, noise to somebody who just wants to run Lawha.
SKILL.md
docs/superpowers/
docs/agents/
.github/copilot-instructions.md

# Obsidian opens a folder as a vault by writing these into it. The project's
# knowledge base lives in a vault one directory up, not in the repo.
.obsidian/
*.base
